    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Your hail claim is underpaid when the insurer accepted it but paid less than it costs to put the property back the way it was. The fix is not a phone call saying the number feels low. It is a line-by-line comparison of the carrier's estimate against the real scope of work, proof for every item that is missing or short, and a written request to re-inspect. If that fails, the policy and Texas law give you further steps, including appraisal.</p>
<p>An underpaid claim is a different problem from a denied one. If the carrier said there was no hail damage at all, or called it wear and tear, read our guide to <a href="/post/denied-hail-damage-insurance-claim/">denied hail damage claims</a> instead. This page assumes the carrier agrees hail hit your property and the argument is about how much.</p>

<h2>Signs your hail claim was underpaid</h2>
<p>None of these proves an underpayment on its own, but two or three together are worth a closer look.</p>
<ul class="checklist">
<li>The payment is a fraction of what two or more roofers bid for the same work.</li>
<li>Only one or two slopes were approved, while the other slopes faced the same storm.</li>
<li>The estimate prices a repair (a few shingles) where a roofer says the shingles can't be repaired.</li>
<li>Gutters, downspouts, vents and A/C fins are dented, but none of them appear on the estimate.</li>
<li>The roof is measured smaller than your roofer's measurement, or the waste factor looks thin.</li>
<li>Interior ceiling stains from the same storm were left off or deferred.</li>
<li>Depreciation is large and you aren't sure whether it is recoverable.</li>
<li>No overhead and profit, even though the job needs a roofer, a gutter crew and a painter.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Read the estimate before you argue with it</h2>
<p>Carrier estimates look dense, but most of the money sits in a few places. Start with the summary page and find five numbers: replacement cost value (RCV), depreciation, recoverable depreciation, your deductible, and the net payment. On a replacement-cost policy, the first check is usually actual cash value: RCV minus depreciation minus the deductible. The withheld depreciation is paid after the work is done, if your policy allows it. On a roof with an ACV endorsement, that depreciation may never come back. Our <a href="/tools/claim-settlement-calculator/">claim settlement calculator</a> walks through the arithmetic with your own figures.</p>
<p>Then go to the roof section and check quantities. A roof is measured in squares (100 square feet each). If the carrier measured 28 squares and your roofer measured 34, that difference flows through every line that depends on area: tear-off, shingles, felt, and disposal. Measurement errors are among the easiest underpayments to prove, because a satellite or drone measurement report settles them.</p>
<p>Finally, look at what is not there. Most underpaid hail claims are short because whole components are missing, not because the shingle price is wrong.</p>

<h2>Line items carriers commonly leave out</h2>
<p>Each of these is a separate line in Xactimate, the estimating software most Texas carriers and public adjusters use. When one is missing, ask why in writing.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Line item</th><th>Why it gets missed</th><th>How to prove it</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Starter strip</td><td>Assumed to be cut from field shingles, or forgotten</td><td>Manufacturer installation instructions that call for starter at eaves and rakes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ridge cap</td><td>Lumped into field shingles instead of priced as its own line</td><td>Photos of the ridge and a linear-foot measurement</td></tr>
<tr><td>Drip edge</td><td>Existing roof never had it, so it isn't "replaced"</td><td>Your city's adopted building code or the permit requirements</td></tr>
<tr><td>Valley metal and flashing</td><td>Not visible from the ground; assumed reusable</td><td>Close-up photos of dented or bent flashing; roofer statement on reuse</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vents, pipe jacks, turbines</td><td>Inspector focused on shingles</td><td>Photos of dents on each vent; a count by type</td></tr>
<tr><td>Soft metals</td><td>Gutters and A/C fins treated as an afterthought</td><td>Chalked dents photographed with a tape measure for scale</td></tr>
<tr><td>Steep and high-roof charges</td><td>Pitch estimated from the ground</td><td>A pitch gauge photo on each slope; story count</td></tr>
<tr><td>Additional layer removal</td><td>Second layer only visible at the edge</td><td>Photo at the drip edge or a lifted tab showing both layers</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ice and water shield, underlayment upgrades</td><td>Left to "supplement later"</td><td>Local code or permit language</td></tr>
<tr><td>Overhead and profit</td><td>Carrier says one trade is involved</td><td>A list of the trades the job needs: roofing, gutters, paint, interior drywall</td></tr>
<tr><td>Interior damage</td><td>Stains appeared after the inspection</td><td>Dated photos, moisture readings, and a link to the storm date</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Not every item applies to every roof. What matters is that the estimate reflects your roof, not a generic one.</p>

<h2>How to request a re-inspection</h2>
<p>A re-inspection is the cheapest fix for an underpaid claim, and carriers grant them routinely when the request is specific. "Please come back out" gets a slow answer. "The estimate omits the north and east slopes, 14 dented roof vents and the second shingle layer; photos attached" gets a scheduled date.</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Put it in writing.</strong> Email the adjuster with your claim number, list each disputed item, and attach photos keyed to that list. Keep a copy of everything you send.</li>
<li><strong>Ask for a roof-level inspection.</strong> If the first inspection was from the ground or by drone only, say so and ask that the adjuster walk the roof or send someone who will.</li>
<li><strong>Have someone there who knows the roof.</strong> Your roofer can point out damage, or a public adjuster can attend on your behalf. Being on the roof together is far more productive than trading photos afterward.</li>
<li><strong>Mark the damage beforehand.</strong> Chalked hail hits and labeled photos make it hard to skip a slope.</li>
<li><strong>Get the result in writing.</strong> Ask for the revised estimate, or a written explanation for each item the carrier still won't pay.</li>
</ol>
<p>Carriers sometimes send an engineer instead of an adjuster. Ask for a copy of the engineer's report when it is finished; it is the document the carrier will rely on. For how carriers test roofs and count hits, see <a href="/post/how-insurance-companies-evaluate-hail-damage/">how insurance companies evaluate hail damage</a>.</p>

<h2>Building a counter-estimate</h2>
<p>A roofer's one-page bid is useful, but it is easy for a carrier to dismiss because it doesn't line up with their estimate. A counter-estimate in Xactimate uses the same pricing database the carrier uses, so the comparison happens line by line, and the argument narrows to specific differences instead of two totals.</p>
<p>A strong counter-estimate has four parts:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The estimate itself</strong>, with every component of the roof system and related exterior and interior damage.</li>
<li><strong>A difference summary</strong> that lists what the carrier left out or under-measured and the dollar effect of each.</li>
<li><strong>Photos keyed to line items</strong>, so the adjuster can match "R&amp;R roof vent, 14 EA" to 14 photos of dented vents.</li>
<li><strong>Supporting documents</strong>: measurement reports, code requirements, manufacturer instructions and storm data for your address.</li>
</ul>
<p>This is the core of what we do as public adjusters. We inspect, measure, write the Xactimate estimate, and send it with the documentation the adjuster needs to approve it.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>The escalation path if the carrier won't move</h2>
<p>Most underpaid claims settle at the re-inspection or counter-estimate stage. When they don't, the next steps run roughly in this order.</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Ask for a supervisor review.</strong> A written request to the adjuster's manager, summarizing the open items, often resolves disputes that stalled with a busy field adjuster.</li>
<li><strong>Invoke appraisal.</strong> If the disagreement is over the amount of loss, most Texas homeowners policies allow either side to demand appraisal. Each side picks an appraiser, and the two appraisers pick an umpire. Read <a href="/post/appraisal-for-hail-damage-claim/">how appraisal works for hail claims</a> before you send a demand.</li>
<li><strong>File a complaint with TDI.</strong> The Texas Department of Insurance accepts consumer complaints about claim handling. A complaint does not set the claim amount, but it puts the carrier's handling of your claim on record with its regulator.</li>
<li><strong>Talk to an attorney.</strong> Some disputes, especially coverage disputes, belong with a lawyer. We work with attorneys when a claim needs one.</li>
</ol>
<div class="callout callout--law"><p><strong>Texas law:</strong> Before filing suit over a weather-related property claim, you must give the insurer written notice at least 61 days in advance stating the amount owed (Tex. Ins. Code §542A.003). If an insurer pays late, Chapter 542 adds penalty interest and attorney's fees (§542.060).</p></div>
<p>Keep the deadlines in view while you dispute. Many private Texas policies set a deadline to report wind and hail damage and a separate deadline to sue. Check the "Duties after loss" and "Suit against us" conditions in your own policy, and see <a href="/post/how-long-do-you-have-to-file-a-hail-claim-in-texas/">how long you have to file a hail claim in Texas</a>.</p>

<h2>Mistakes that make underpayments harder to fix</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Replacing the roof before the dispute is documented.</strong> Once the old shingles are in a dumpster, so is your evidence. Photograph everything first, and tell the carrier before work starts.</li>
<li><strong>Signing a document you haven't read.</strong> Depositing an undisputed payment is not usually the same as agreeing it is the final amount, but a signed release can be. Read anything labeled "release" or "final" carefully.</li>
<li><strong>Arguing totals instead of items.</strong> "Your estimate is $9,000 short" invites a flat no. "Your estimate omits these eleven items" invites a revision.</li>
<li><strong>Letting the roofer negotiate the claim.</strong> Texas law bars a contractor from acting as your public adjuster on property they are repairing or may repair (§4102.163). Your roofer can document damage and give you a bid; the negotiation is yours or a licensed adjuster's.</li>
</ul>

<h2>When a public adjuster makes sense</h2>
<p>If the gap between the carrier's estimate and the real cost is small, you can often close it yourself with the steps above. If the gap is large, several slopes or the whole repair-or-replace decision is in dispute, or you've already asked twice and been told no, a public adjuster can take over the inspection, estimate and negotiation. Our fee is a percentage of the claim payment, Texas law caps it at 10%, and there is no upfront cost. Start with a <a href="/services/denied-underpaid-claims/">free review of your underpaid claim</a>.</p>
